  "resolved": {
   "B6": "[E] (13) subtracts maturing deposits from cash while [E] 2.2.3 says the same amounts are reinvested. Taken literally the deposit base would run off completely and the bank would fail, so the two cannot both hold. We reinvest (deposit_cash_rule='reinvest'): maturing deposits move no cash, credited interest raises the liability and lowers equity, and only the growth is new cash. 'literal' reproduces the printed formula.",
   "B7": "[E] (14) defines the cash penalty as (C-30MR)+ (min(1/D,1)-1), which is zero or NEGATIVE, and (15) then subtracts it, so negative rates would pay the bank. The sign inside is wrong. We use (C-30MR)+ (1-min(1/D,1)) >= 0 ('corrected'); 'literal' reproduces the printed formula.",
   "B8": "[E] (11a)-(11b) cap customer deposit rates but do not floor them, so on a start date with negative rates the bank charges its depositors. [E] 1 itself notes that Swiss customer rates 'were often floored at 0%'. We keep the formulas as printed (deposit_rate_floor=None) and leave the floor as an option, because the omission is the paper's, not a typo. It is worth about 17 mCHF a year at the start and fades as rates rise."
  }
